{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And Balaam said to Balak's officers.</b> He does not mention the elders of Midian, because Balak is the principal and he is the one who sent to summon him." "<b>For HaShem refused to let me go with you.</b> There is no desire of HaShem for this mission at all, for Balaam's thought was that HaShem would not permit him to go at all — not even with officers more numerous and distinguished than the first ones." "<b>And behold.</b> Balak suspected him of having said this on his own in order to increase his fee, and this is what is written: ״ויוסף עוד בלק שלוח שרים רבים ונכבדים מאלה״ — Balak again sent officers more numerous and distinguished than these — to show him that he greatly desired him, in order to give him wealth and riches according to all that he might decree upon him. And Balaam answered them: ״בכל ממונו לא אוכל לעבור את פי ה׳״ — with all his wealth I cannot transgress the word of HaShem — for He is my Elohim and I cannot do small or great if I transgress His word." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> ״כי מאן ה׳ לתתי להלוך עמכם״ — for HaShem refused to let me go with you — but with great officers. From this you learn that there were in him three traits: an evil eye, a haughty spirit, and a greedy soul. An evil eye, as it is written (Numbers 24:2): ״וישא בלעם את עיניו וירא את ישראל שוכן לשבטיו״; a haughty spirit, as it is written: ״כי מאן ה׳ לתתי להלוך עמכם״; a greedy soul, as it is written: ״אם יתן לי בלק מלא ביתו כסף וזהב״. And from this we learn that he coveted others' money, saying: It is fitting that he give me all the silver and gold that he has, for he needs to hire troops, and perhaps he prevails and perhaps he does not prevail, whereas I surely prevail."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:04.073274348Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}